\description{
These methods report a \code{netreg}, the result of \code{\link[=estimate_mrqap]{estimate_mrqap()}},
whether its class is \code{netreg_lm}, \code{netreg_glm}, or \code{netreg_css}.
\itemize{
\item \code{print()} shows the result as a report: how the null distribution was
made, the estimates, and the permutation p-values.
\item \code{summary()} gives the full table of the coefficients: the columns of
\code{tidy()} and the parts of each term.
\item \code{tidy()} and \code{glance()} give the results as a tibble, after \code{{broom}}:
one row for each coefficient, and one row for the whole model.
So an MRQAP result can sit in one table beside an ERGM or a SIENA
one (see \link{other_results}).
\item \code{nobs()} gives the number of dyads that the model used:
unobserved dyads, fixed ties, and the loops that the model leaves out
are not counted.
\item \code{logLik()} and \code{deviance()} give the log-likelihood and the deviance
of the observed model, so \code{AIC()} and \code{BIC()} work too.
\item \code{fitted()} and \code{residuals()} give one value for each dyad that the
model used, in the rows of the dyad-level data.
\item \code{formula()} gives the formula that was fitted, after \code{\link[=specify_mrqap]{specify_mrqap()}}
read it, and \code{terms()} gives the terms of the columns that were fitted,
as the coefficients name them.
For the formula of chosen rows of a table of terms, see \link{specify_terms}.
}
