\details{
The p-values come from the permutations. Every other method reads the
observed model, which the permutations do not change.
A permutation test gives a p-value but no standard error,
so \code{tidy()} and \code{summary()} have no \code{std.error} column.
\code{p.value} is read from the permutations of the test statistic,
as \code{print()} shows it.
When the predictors are permuted, the intercept has no test,
and its \code{p.value} is \code{NA}.

Two methods of \code{{stats}} work without a method here:
\code{coef()} gives the estimates, and \code{update()} fits the model again,
with a changed formula, \code{times}, or \code{control}, from the call that the
result keeps.
}
\section{Methods left out}{

\itemize{
\item \code{vcov()} and \code{confint()}: a covariance or an interval from the observed
model would not come from the permutations, and could be read as the
basis of the permutation p-values.
\item \code{weights()}: a network regression uses no weights.
\item \code{[}: a result has no rows to select. Use \code{$} or \code{[[} for an element.
\item \code{profile()}: a permutation fit has no likelihood to profile.
}
}

\section{Fixed effects}{

A gaussian model absorbs its fixed effects by demeaning. Its
log-likelihood and deviance are those of the model with a dummy for each
group, and \code{logLik()} counts the absorbed parameters in its \code{df}, so
that \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()}, and \code{glance()} are those of that model too.
\code{fitted()} gives the observed values less the residuals, not the
demeaned fitted values.
}

